# Configs v@PROJECT_VERSION@

## Introduction

Configs is a C++ library designed to simplify project configuration management. It allows you to define configuration fields directly inside C++ structs, associate each field with a key, and automatically load values from different sources.

The library supports four configuration sources:

- **YAML** – Load configurations from YAML files.
- **JSON** – Load configurations from JSON files.
- **ENV** – Load configurations from environment variables or `.env` files.
- **Args** – Load configurations from command-line arguments.

Configs supports nested objects, vectors, and default values. It uses recursive loading, eliminating the need to implement separate configuration loader classes and methods in your projects.

The library is designed for CMake-based C++ projects and can easily be integrated into an existing codebase as a Git submodule.

## Getting Started

1. Add Configs to your project as a Git submodule.
2. Configure the required dependencies and build options.
3. Include the main header file: `#include <configs.hpp>`.
4. Define your configuration structs and load values from your preferred source.

### Build Steps

#### 1. Add Configs as a Git submodule

```bash
cd <project-directory>
git submodule add <repo-url> external/Configs
git submodule update --init
cd external/Configs
git checkout v@PROJECT_VERSION@
```

#### 2. Configure the project

Configs uses CMake and supports enabling or disabling each configuration source independently.

```cmake
-DYAML_ENABLE=true
-DJSON_ENABLE=true
-DENV_ENABLE=true
-DARGS_ENABLE=true
```

Make sure the dependencies required by the enabled sources are available:

| Source | Dependency |
|---|---|
| YAML | yaml-cpp |
| JSON | nlohmann_json |
| ENV | libenvpp |
| Args | CLI11 |

#### 3. Include Configs in your project

For example, using `ExternalProject_Add`:

```cmake
include(ExternalProject)

ExternalProject_Add(Configs
    SOURCE_DIR ${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/external/Configs
    BINARY_DIR ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/configs_build
    CONFIGURE_COMMAND ""
    BUILD_COMMAND ""
    INSTALL_COMMAND ""
)

target_include_directories(${PROJECT_NAME}
    PRIVATE ${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/external/Configs/include
)
```

Enable the required configuration sources through compile definitions:

```cmake
target_compile_definitions(${PROJECT_NAME} PRIVATE
    YAML_ENABLE=false
    JSON_ENABLE=false
    ENV_ENABLE=false
    ARGS_ENABLE=true
)
```

## Usage

### Define configuration structs

Here is an example of a configuration structure containing scalar values, vectors, nested objects, and default values.

my_config.hpp :
```cpp
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <filesystem>

#include <configs.hpp>


struct Person
{
    configs::ConfigItem <std::string>   name { "name" };
    configs::ConfigItem <int>           age { "age" };

    DECLARE_MEMBERS(&Person::name, &Person::age)
};


struct MyConfig
{
    configs::ConfigItem <std::string>                          string_value{ "string_value" };
    configs::ConfigItem <int>                                  int_value{ "int_value" };
    configs::ConfigItem <bool>                                 boolean_value{ "boolean_value" };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::filesystem::path>                path_value{ "path_value" };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::vector<std::string>>             vector_string{ "vector_string" };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::vector<std::filesystem::path>>   vector_path{ "vector_path" };
    configs::ConfigItem <Person>                               object_person{ "object_person" };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::vector<Person>>                  vector_person{ "vector_person" };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::string>                          str_with_default{ "str_def", std::string("None") };
    configs::ConfigItem <int>                                  int_with_default{ "int_def", 1000 };
    configs::ConfigItem <bool>                                 bool_with_default{ "bool_def", true };
    configs::ConfigItem <Person>                               person_with_default{ "person_def", {std::string("Ali"), 20} };
    configs::ConfigItem <std::vector<int>>                     vector_with_default{ "vector_def", {101,102,103} };

    DECLARE_MEMBERS(&MyConfig::string_value,
        &MyConfig::int_value,
        &MyConfig::boolean_value,
        &MyConfig::path_value,
        &MyConfig::vector_string,
        &MyConfig::vector_path,
        &MyConfig::object_person,
        &MyConfig::vector_person,
        &MyConfig::str_with_default,
        &MyConfig::int_with_default,
        &MyConfig::bool_with_default,
        &MyConfig::person_with_default,
        &MyConfig::vector_with_default)
};
```

### Load from YAML

`config.yaml`:

```yaml
myapp_config:
    string_value: "hello world!"
    int_value: 123
    boolean_value: true
    path_value: "/usr/bin"
    vector_string: ["str1", "str2", "str3"]
    vector_path: ["/usr/path1", "/usr/path2"]
    object_person:
        name: "ali"
        age: 51
    vector_person:
        - name: "reza"
          age: 52
        - name: "omid"
          age: 53
```

`main.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "my_config.hpp"

int main()
{
    configs::ConfigItem<MyConfig> config { "myapp_config" };

    config.load_from_<configs::SourceType::Yaml>("config.yaml");

    auto user = config().vector_person()[1];

    std::cout << "Name: " << user.name()
              << " - Age: " << user.age() << std::endl;

    // Output: Name: reza - Age: 52

    return 0;
}
```

### Load from JSON

`config.json`:

```json
{
    "myapp_config": {
        "string_value": "hello world!",
        "int_value": 123,
        "boolean_value": true,
        "path_value": "/usr/bin",
        "vector_string": ["str1", "str2", "str3"],
        "vector_path": ["/usr/path1", "/usr/path2"],
        "object_person": {
            "name": "ali",
            "age": 51
        },
        "vector_person": [
            { "name": "reza", "age": 52 },
            { "name": "omid", "age": 53 }
        ]
    }
}
```

Load the configuration:

```cpp
config.load_from_<configs::SourceType::Json>("config.json");
```

### Load from ENV

`.env`:

```env
MYAPP_CONFIG_STRING_VALUE=hello world!
MYAPP_CONFIG_INT_VALUE=123
MYAPP_CONFIG_BOOLEAN_VALUE=true
MYAPP_CONFIG_PATH_VALUE=/usr/bin
MYAPP_CONFIG_VECTOR_STRING=str1;str2;str3;
MYAPP_CONFIG_VECTOR_PATH=/usr/path1;/usr/path2;
MYAPP_CONFIG_OBJECT_PERSON_NAME=ali
MYAPP_CONFIG_OBJECT_PERSON_AGE=51
MYAPP_CONFIG_VECTOR_PERSON=reza,52;omid,53;
```

Load the configuration:

```cpp
config.load_from_<configs::SourceType::Env>("");
```

### Load from command-line arguments

Configs also supports loading configuration values directly from command-line arguments using CLI11.

For example:

```cpp
int main(int argc, char** argv)
{
    configs::ConfigItem<MyConfig> config { "myapp_config" };

    config.load_from_args(argc, argv);

    auto user = config().vector_person()[1];

    std::cout << "Name: " << user.name()
              << " - Age: " << user.age() << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

Example command-line arguments:

```bash
./myapp \
    --myapp-config-string-value "hello world!" \
    --myapp-config-int-value 123 \
    --myapp-config-boolean-value true \
    --myapp-config-path-value /usr/bin
    --myapp-config-vector-string str1 str2 str3 \
    --myapp-config-vector-int 11 12 13 \
    --myapp-config-vector-path /usr/path1 /usr/path2 \
    --myapp-config-object-person ali 51 \
    --myapp-config-vector-person reza 52 omid 53
```

The command-line option names are derived from the configuration keys. Nested objects and vectors are also supported.

